World model: Clicking orange at (9,49) removed orange node id 59 and added a new orange node id 57, keeping the total orange count at 15. No other colors changed. Goal model: Still unclear – orange objects persist despite clicks; perhaps the goal is to eliminate a specific orange shape (maybe the one with id 59) or to move orange to a target region. Open questions: Does clicking a different orange (maybe on the edge) reduce count? Does clicking a non‑orange (e.g., a gray G) affect orange? Plan: Test clicking a gray (G) tile to see if orange count changes.
{"code":"g_nodes=[n for n in current_frame.segmentation['nodes'] if n['color']=='G']
print('G count',len(g_nodes))
for n in g_nodes[:5]:
    print('G id',n['id'],'boundary',n['boundary'][0])"}

World model: Clicking any orange tile swaps one orange object for another (removing one id, adding another) but the total number and overall distribution (average position) stay the same. Clicking magenta or pink or white also does not change counts. No reduction in any colored objects observed yet.
Goal model: Likely need to eliminate a specific orange object type – perhaps the orange that is adjacent to a magenta or gray tile, or to create a configuration where orange disappears after a certain pattern.
Open questions: Which interaction triggers removal of orange without replacement? Maybe clicking a gray (G) tile that is adjacent to orange will cause orange to vanish.
Plan: Identify a gray tile that touches an orange tile and click it.
{"code":"# find gray nodes adjacent to orange via adjacency list
orange_ids={n['id'] for n in current_frame.segmentation['nodes'] if n['color']=='O'}
gray_nodes=[n for n in current_frame.segmentation['nodes'] if n['color']=='G']
adj= current_frame.segmentation['adjacency_list']
# build adjacency dict
adj_dict={i:[] for i,_ in adj}
for i,j in adj:
    adj_dict[i].append(j)
    adj_dict[j].append(i)
# find gray adjacent to orange
candidates=[]
for g in gray_nodes:
    for nb in adj_dict.get(g['id'],[]):
        if nb in orange_ids:
            candidates.append(g)
            break
print('candidates',len(candidates))
if candidates:
    print('first gray id',candidates[0]['id'],'pos',candidates[0]['boundary'][0])
"}

